[126.139, "o", "Justine dispatched in background. Starting Phase 1: Doc-to-Implementation Audit.\r\n\r\nLet me read the project docs to extract testable claims. The key documents are the README and the SKILL.md.\r\n"]
[126.159, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m\u254c\u254c\u254c context: 104,084 tokens (cache read: 103,885, write: 198, output: 4) \u254c\u254c\u254c\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[126.242, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u23bf Read\u001b[0m \u001b[2mREADME.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[126.272, "o", "  \u001b[2mRead: README.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[126.282, "o", "\u001b[2m    1\u2192# Holtz\r\n         2\u2192\r\n         3\u2192A Claude Code plugin that audits your entire codebase, documents every defect in a structured punchlist, fixes them with TDD, and then does it again. And again. Until two consecutive passes find nothing new. You will think your code is clean. Holtz will disagree. He will keep disagreeing until he can't anymore, and then he will stop.\r\n         4\u2192\r\n         5\u2192He will not apologize for any of it.\r\n         6\u2192\r\n         7\u2192He works with a second auditor named Justine, who runs in parallel using a different methodology. She's breadth-first where he's depth-first. They don't coordinate during the audit. They merge after, and what each one missed becomes a map of the other's blind spots.\r\n         8\u2192\r\n         9\u2192## Why this exists\r\n        10\u2192\r\n        11\u2192Code review happens once. The comments get addressed, the reviewer approves, everyone moves on. But fixing a bug changes the terrain. A fix in module A shifts an assumption in module B, and now there's a new bug that didn't exist ten minutes ago. Nobody goes back to check. The PR is merged. CI is green. The bug ships.\r\n        12\u2192\r\n        13\u2192Holtz goes back to check.\r\n        14\u2192\r\n        15\u2192He runs a seven-phase audit and then starts over. Finds what the fixes uncovered. Fixes those too. Runs again. This continues until two consecutive passes produce zero new findings across nine analytical lenses. That's convergence. Everything before that is just progress.\r\n    \u001b[2m    ... (85 more lines)\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[126.287, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u23bf Read\u001b[0m \u001b[2mREADME.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[126.304, "o", "  \u001b[2mRead: README.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[126.314, "o", "\u001b[2m    100\u2192## Extending Holtz\r\n       101\u2192\r\n       102\u2192Holtz is built to be added to.\r\n       103\u2192\r\n       104\u2192**Lenses.** The nine analytical lenses that ship are defaults. Add any lens to the registry file and it joins the convergence rotation. If you've found a way of looking at code that catches things the existing lenses miss, that's the kind of contribution that makes the registry better for everyone.\r\n       105\u2192\r\n       106\u2192**Patterns.** Each seed pattern is a markdown file with a YAML header, a description, an executable detection heuristic, and an example. Write one for a bug class you keep seeing. If the heuristic fires during recon, Holtz starts the audit already knowing what to look for.\r\n       107\u2192\r\n       108\u2192**Edge types.** The seven edge types in the impact graph cover most relationships, but if your domain has a relationship type that `assumes` and `diverges_from` don't capture \u2014 temporal ordering, permission scoping, schema versioning \u2014 the graph model supports extension.\r\n       109\u2192\r\n       110\u2192PRs with new lenses, patterns, or edge types are welcome. The whole point of the pattern library is that it gets better as more codebases get audited.\r\n       111\u2192\r\n       112\u2192## The seven phases\r\n       113\u2192\r\n       114\u2192**Phase 0: Recon.** Project structure, test infrastructure, baseline metrics, lint, git churn, skipped tests, mutation scanning, architecture drift, predictive recon. Eight steps, each written to disk immediately. By the time Phase 1 starts, Holtz has a map.\r\n    \u001b[2m    ... (99 more lines)\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[127.703, "o", "Now let me verify the key testable claims from the README. Starting with HIGH-priority predicted areas.\r\n"]
